    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-110307",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table",
      "problem": "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$  of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| $-3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=-3,$ $g(4)=3,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{3}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
