    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-110497",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={3}^{x}-1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$",
      "worked_solution": "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x}-1 & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{x} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
